Okay, let's see. Oliver has to put away books at the library. There are 46 books total. But a librarian takes 10 of them, so Oliver is left with 46 minus 10. Let me calculate that first. 46 minus 10 is 36. So Oliver has 36 books to put away.

Now, the problem says he can fit 4 books on a shelf. I need to figure out how many shelves he needs. Hmm, so if each shelf holds 4 books, how many shelves does it take to hold 36 books? 

I think I need to divide the total number of books by the number of books per shelf. So 36 divided by 4. Let me do that division. 36 divided by 4 is 9. Wait, that seems straightforward. But wait, let me check if there's any catch here. 

If it divides evenly, then 9 shelves would be exactly enough. If there was a remainder, we would need an extra shelf. For example, if it was 37 books, 37 divided by 4 is 9 with a remainder of 1, so he would need 10 shelves. But here, 36 divided by 4 is exactly 9. So no remainder. Therefore, Oliver needs 9 shelves. 

Let me just go through the steps again to be sure. Total books: 46. Librarian takes 10, so 46 - 10 = 36. Each shelf holds 4 books. 36 divided by 4 is 9. Yep, that seems right. I don't think I made a mistake here. So the answer should be 9 shelves.
